module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.PermissibleBasisChartData
public import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Standard
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AlgebraicIndependent.Basic

/-! # Triangular coordinates for the projective order algebra

Source: Hironaka, Certain numerical characters of singularities (1970),
§13, (13.2), p.168, and the polynomial expansion in Lemma 18, p.174.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-triangular-coordinates).

The pivot labels are 1 through e. The free labels are e+1 through r together
with 0. Keeping these as subtypes retains the existing projective coordinates
and dehomogenization at zero, including the empty-pivot case.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace Hironaka

/-- Labels of the pivot coordinates, with the paper's one-based indexing.
Source: Hironaka (1970), (13.2), p.168.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-triangular-coordinates). -/
abbrev OrderPivot (r e : ℕ) := {i : Fin (r + 1) // 0 < i.val ∧ i.val ≤ e}

/-- Labels of the remaining variables, including the distinguished variable zero.
Source: Hironaka (1970), Lemma 18, p.174.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-triangular-coordinates). -/
abbrev OrderFreeVariable (r e : ℕ) := {i : Fin (r + 1) // i = 0 ∨ e < i.val}

/-- The weighted degree q dot A plus the ordinary degree of the bounded monomial.
Source: Hironaka (1970), Lemma 18, p.174.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-triangular-coordinates). -/
def orderExpansionWeight {r e : ℕ} (q A β : OrderPivot r e → ℕ) : ℕ :=
  (∑ i, q i * A i) + ∑ i, β i

/-- Triangular generators, algebraic independence, and the unique homogeneous
bounded-monomial expansion for the projective order algebra. The permutation
fixes zero; its restriction reorders both the projective and affine labels.

Source: Hironaka (1970), (13.2), p.168; Lemma 18, p.174.
(blueprint: lem-trf-projective-order-triangular-coordinates).

Proof (the seven steps of the accepted DAG split):
1. Set A=k[t] and invert X0. Substitution Xi=ti*u, X0=u identifies B[X0^-1]
with A[u,u^-1]. The inverse image of p in this ring is homogeneous prime q,
misses X0, and B_q identifies with (L[u,u^-1]) localized at N[u,u^-1].
2. For g in L, membership in N^d after this extension and localization is
membership in N^d already: a denominator outside N[u,u^-1] has a unit
coefficient, and coefficientwise membership lets us cancel that unit. A form
of degree d maps to u^d*delta(f), so the U_d agree with those of
lem-trf-projective-order-algebra-additive-generators. That result makes U the
algebra generated by its positive-degree homogeneous additive elements.
3. These additive elements form spaces Q_d. By
lem-trf-additive-polynomial-monomial-classification they are sums of pure
powers of degree 1 in characteristic zero or a characteristic power otherwise.
Frobenius powers preserve them. No nonzero c*X0^d belongs to Q_d since its
dehomogenization is a unit in L.
4. Intersect Q_d successively with k[Xi,...,Xr,X0]. Choose the least nonzero
degree, permute a nonzero non-X0 coefficient into position i, and normalize it
to one. The resulting sigma_i has the displayed triangular shape. Residual
spaces decrease, so the degrees are nondecreasing. At most r pivots occur,
since at the end only k[X0] remains and its residual spaces vanish.
5. An additive form of degree d in the i-th residual space has d/q_i a
characteristic power (or 1 in characteristic zero). Subtract its Xi^d
coefficient times sigma_i^(d/q_i). This moves it to the next residual space.
Induction exhausts all additive generators, proving U=k[sigma], also for e=0.
6. In lexicographic order X1>...>Xr>X0 each sigma_i has leading monomial
Xi^q_i with coefficient one. The leading exponents of sigma^A X^beta W^gamma
are (q_i*A_i+beta_i,gamma). Quotient and remainder by q_i make these distinct
for bounded beta. A largest leading term cannot cancel in a finite relation.
This proves linear independence, and beta=0 proves algebraic independence.
7. Replace Xi^q_i by sigma_i minus its later-variable terms. Pivot exponents
decrease lexicographically at bounded total weighted degree, so the reduction
terminates. Grouping terms gives the expansion and its coefficient degrees.
The independence in step 6 gives uniqueness. Empty tuples, r=0, and d=0 are
included throughout. -/
theorem projective_order_triangular_coordinates
    {k : Type u} [Field k] (r : ℕ)
    (p : PrimeSpectrum (MvPolynomial {i : Fin (r + 1) // i ≠ 0} k)) :
    ∃ π : Equiv.Perm (Fin (r + 1)), π 0 = 0 ∧
    ∃ e : ℕ, e ≤ r ∧
    ∃ q : OrderPivot r e → ℕ,
    ∃ c : OrderPivot r e → Fin (r + 1) → k,
    ∃ σ : OrderPivot r e → MvPolynomial (Fin (r + 1)) k,
      (∀ i, 1 ≤ q i) ∧
      (∀ i j, i.val.val ≤ j.val.val → q i ≤ q j) ∧
      (∀ i, σ i = MvPolynomial.X (π i.val) ^ q i +
        ∑ j ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun j : Fin (r + 1) ↦ i.val.val < j.val ∨ j = 0),
          MvPolynomial.C (c i j) * MvPolynomial.X (π j) ^ q i) ∧
      (∀ f : MvPolynomial (Fin (r + 1)) k,
        (∀ d : ℕ, InOrderSpace r p d (MvPolynomial.homogeneousComponent d f)) ↔
          f ∈ Algebra.adjoin k (Set.range σ)) ∧
      (ringChar k = 0 → ∀ i, q i = 1) ∧
      (ringChar k ≠ 0 → ∀ i, ∃ a : ℕ, q i = ringChar k ^ a) ∧
      AlgebraicIndependent k
        (Sum.elim σ (fun j : OrderFreeVariable r e ↦ MvPolynomial.X (π j.val))) ∧
      (∀ (d : ℕ) (f : MvPolynomial (Fin (r + 1)) k), f.IsHomogeneous d →
        ∃! F : ((OrderPivot r e → ℕ) ×
            {β : OrderPivot r e → ℕ // ∀ i, β i < q i}) →₀
              MvPolynomial (OrderFreeVariable r e) k,
          (∀ aβ, (F aβ).IsHomogeneous
              (d - orderExpansionWeight q aβ.1 aβ.2.val) ∧
            (d < orderExpansionWeight q aβ.1 aβ.2.val → F aβ = 0)) ∧
          f = F.sum (fun aβ g ↦
            MvPolynomial.aeval
                (fun j : OrderFreeVariable r e ↦ MvPolynomial.X (π j.val)) g *
              (∏ i, σ i ^ aβ.1 i) *
              ∏ i, MvPolynomial.X (π i.val) ^ aβ.2.val i)) := by
  sorry

end Hironaka
